## Why This Is Asked
This tests practical serverless wiring, least-privilege IAM, and cross-account access basics.

## Key Concepts
- Lambda IAM roles, API Gateway integration, S3 PutObject, SSE-KMS, cross-account roles.
- KMS key policies and bucket policies.
- End-to-end testing of write and cross-account read.

## Code Example
```javascript
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
const s3 = new AWS.S3();
exports.handler = async (event) => {
  const payload = JSON.stringify(event);
  await s3.putObject({
    Bucket: process.env.LOG_BUCKET,
    Key: `logs/${Date.now()}.json`,
    Body: payload,
    ServerSideEncryption: 'aws:kms'
  }).promise();
  return { statusCode: 200, body: 'ok' };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test failure modes (kms failure, bucket policy denial)?
- How would you rotate the KMS key and propagate changes?

## Why This Is Asked

This validates practical isolation in a shared data lake, ensuring least privilege and scalable onboarding. It also tests policy layering and auditing capabilities.

## Key Concepts

- IAM roles per tenant with strict resource boundaries
- Trust policies tied to the tenant app identity
- S3 prefix-scoped permissions with bucket or IAM policy conditions
- Onboarding automation for new tenants and centralized governance
- Cross-tenant test plans and auditing hooks

## Code Example

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:ListBucket"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::corp-data-lake",
      "Condition": {"StringLike": {"s3:prefix": "tenant-<tid>/*"}}
    },
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:GetObject","s3:PutObject"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::corp-data-lake/tenant-<tid>/*",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id": "<tid>"}}
    }
  ]
}
```

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["sts:AssumeRole"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:iam::<account>:role/TenantRole-<tid>",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id": "<tid>"}}
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend this model to support cross-region backups without cross-tenant data leakage?
- How would you automate onboarding for new tenants without touching code (CI/CD policy issuance, audit log integration)?

## Why This Is Asked
This evaluates designing scalable, secure, cross-account data access across tenants with strong isolation and auditable sharing.

## Key Concepts
- Lake Formation catalog-based permissions
- S3 per-tenant prefixes and object ownership
- KMS key policies for residency and encryption
- IAM SCPs and cross-account sharing, data marts

## Code Example
```javascript
// Tenant user policy (pseudo)
{
  'Version': '2012-10-17',
  'Statement': [
    {'Effect': 'Allow', 'Action': ['lakeformation:GrantPermissions', 's3:GetObject'], 'Resource': ['arn:aws:s3:::corp-data/tenant-id-*/**']}
  ]
}

// Data-mart role policy (pseudo)
{
  'Version': '2012-10-17',
  'Statement': [
    {'Effect': 'Allow', 'Action': ['lakeformation:SelectFromDataCatalog'], 'Resource': '*'}
  ]
}

// Cross-account share policy (pseudo)
{
  'Version': '2012-10-17',
  'Statement': [
    {'Effect': 'Allow', 'Action': ['glue:GetTable','s3:GetObject'], 'Resource': ['arn:aws:glue:*:*:catalog','arn:aws:s3:::shared-data/**']}
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you monitor and alert on policy drift?
- What are the trade-offs of using Lake Formation versus direct IAM policies for data access?

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates your ability to design a scalable, multi-tenant data ingestion architecture across AWS accounts while addressing security, reliability, and cost optimization challenges.

## Key Concepts

- Cross-account IAM roles with trust relationships
- Tenant isolation through data partitioning and access controls
- Exactly-once processing using idempotent operations
- Kinesis Data Streams, Lambda, Firehose, and S3 integration
- KMS encryption and data retention policies
- Cost management through throttling and lifecycle strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Idempotent processor using DynamoDB for deduplication
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
const dynamodb = new AWS.DynamoDB.DocumentClient();

exports.handler = async (event) => {
  const failures = [];

  for (const record of event.Records) {
    const { tenantId, sequenceId, data } = JSON.parse(record.kinesis.data);
    const tableName = process.env.DDB_TABLE;

    try {
      // Check if sequence already processed
      const result = await dynamodb.get({
        TableName: tableName,
        Key: { tenantId, sequenceId }
      }).promise();

      if (result.Item) {
        continue; // Skip duplicate
      }

      // Process data...
      await processData(tenantId, data);

      // Mark as processed
      await dynamodb.put({
        TableName: tableName,
        Item: { tenantId, sequenceId, processedAt: Date.now() }
      }).promise();

    } catch (error) {
      failures.push({ itemIdentifier: record.kinesis.sequenceNumber });
    }
  }

  return { batchItemFailures: failures };
};
```
